// 31 · Peer-Led Education: why the message lands differently from a teammate.
// Composition: hook = one plain statement across the slide; takeaway = the question is the
// whole slide; alignment = right (headlines set right, hero hugging the right margin).
// Problem = full-height soft panel on the left third; application = three unnumbered lines.

const R = 12.58; // right edge of the content box

module.exports = {
  num: 31,
  slug: "peer-led-education",
  title: "Peer-Led Education",
  category: "Leadership & Culture",
  palette: "forest",
  hero: { layout: "slope-chart", concept: "Four roles, two points each: how often players and coaches named the captain as the team's best leader, against how often they did not (Fransen et al. 2014). The teammate athletes follow is often not the one with the title." },
  compose: { hook: "statement", takeaway: "question-only", align: "right" },
  oneIdea: "Teammates can carry a message staff can't, if we pick the ones the team already follows and back them with a script and an expert.",
  question: "Who on your team would carry this message better than you?",
  caveats: `Keep the peer-led claim modest. Cuijpers (2002) pooled only 12 school drug-prevention studies of uneven quality: peer-led programs came out somewhat ahead on average (d = 0.24), and some studies favored adult leaders. ATLAS was delivered by coaches and student team leaders together, with research staff running the weight-room sessions; ATHENA was peer-led but explicitly scripted and built into practice. Neither is "hand it to the players." Both were high school programs; we have no college trial of the same design.

The slope chart is Fransen et al.'s captain data redrawn. The right-hand points are simply 100 minus the left: "not the captain" covers another player and also the teams where respondents said no one filled that role. It shows that the title and the influence often sit with different people. It does not compare a coach's message with a teammate's; no study here measured that directly. The sample was Flemish club and school-age to adult team sports, not NCAA.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "fransen2014", cite: "Fransen K, Vanbeselaere N, De Cuyper B, Vande Broek G, Boen F. The myth of the team captain as principal leader: extending the athlete leadership classification within sport teams. J Sports Sci. 2014;32(14):1389-1397. doi:10.1080/02640414.2014.891291", grade: "cross-sectional survey (4,451 players and coaches, nine team sports, Belgium)", found: "external", supports: "Captain named the primary task leader by 31.7% of participants, motivational 24.6%, social 15.5%, external 10.1%; 44% did not see the captain as the principal leader in any of the four roles; only 1% saw the captain as best in all four. 'Informal leaders rather than the captain take the lead, both on and off the field.'" },
    { id: "cuijpers2002", cite: "Cuijpers P. Peer-led and adult-led school drug prevention: a meta-analytic comparison. J Drug Educ. 2002;32(2):107-119. doi:10.2190/LPN9-KBDC-HPVB-JPTM", grade: "meta-analysis of 12 studies of modest quality", found: "external", supports: "Peer-led programs were somewhat more effective than the same programs led by adults (d = 0.24), with large differences between studies." },
    { id: "goldberg1996", cite: "Goldberg L, Elliot D, Clarke GN, et al. Effects of a multidimensional anabolic steroid prevention intervention: the Adolescents Training and Learning to Avoid Steroids (ATLAS) Program. JAMA. 1996;276(19):1555-1562. doi:10.1001/jama.1996.03540190027025", grade: "randomized prospective trial (31 high school football teams)", found: "external", supports: "Sessions delivered by coaches and student team leaders improved knowledge, refusal skills, nutrition and exercise behaviors, belief in the team as an information source, and lowered intent to use steroids, sustained at one year." },
    { id: "elliot2004", cite: "Elliot DL, Goldberg L, Moe EL, Defrancesco CA, Durham MB, Hix-Small H. Preventing substance use and disordered eating: initial outcomes of the ATHENA (Athletes Targeting Healthy Exercise and Nutrition Alternatives) program. Arch Pediatr Adolesc Med. 2004;158(11):1043-1049. doi:10.1001/archpedi.158.11.1043", grade: "prospective controlled trial, randomized by school (928 athletes, 40 teams)", found: "external", supports: "A scripted, peer-led curriculum built into team practice reduced diet pill use and new use of performance-enhancing substances in female high school athletes." },
    { id: "fransen2020", cite: "Fransen K, Haslam SA, Steffens NK, Mallett CJ, Peters K, Boen F. Making 'us' better: high-quality athlete leadership relates to health and burnout in professional Australian football teams. Eur J Sport Sci. 2020;20(7):953-963. doi:10.1080/17461391.2019.1680736", grade: "cross-sectional study (120 professional athletes, three teams)", found: "external", supports: "Having a good athlete leader on the team was associated with better health and lower burnout, through a stronger shared team identity." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Think about the last time a message really stuck with an athlete. A lot of the time it didn't come from us. It came from a teammate, in the locker room or on the bus, in words we would never have chosen.

That isn't a knock on staff. It's how people work, especially at nineteen. Today is about using that on purpose instead of hoping it happens.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The same message lands differently from a teammate.", { x: R - 11.2, y: 2.15, w: 11.2, h: 2.4, size: 62, align: "right" });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Most of what we do in education goes one way: staff stand up, athletes listen.

The research on letting peers lead is encouraging but not a slam dunk. A meta-analysis of twelve school drug-prevention studies found peer-led versions did a little better than the same program led by adults, and results swung widely from study to study.

The best sport examples are ATLAS and ATHENA. They were team programs, run inside practice, led by players with coaches alongside, and fully scripted. They changed real behaviors: less steroid intent in football players, less diet pill use in female athletes. Peers plus structure, not peers instead of staff.`,
      build(s, { STAGE }) {
        s.rect(0, 0, STAGE.w / 3, STAGE.h, { fill: "soft" });
        const px = 0.75, pw = 3.45;
        s.label("Peer-led vs. adult-led", { x: px, y: 1.95, w: pw });
        s.text(px, 2.35, pw, 1.3, "Slightly better on average across 12 studies. Results varied widely.", { font: "light", size: 20, lineH: 1.2 });
        s.source("Cuijpers 2002", { x: px, y: 3.62, w: pw, size: 12, valign: "top", refs: ["cuijpers2002"] });
        s.label("Team programs", { x: px, y: 4.3, w: pw });
        s.text(px, 4.7, pw, 1.0, "Scripted, led by players with coaches, built into practice.", { font: "light", size: 20, lineH: 1.2 });
