module.exports = {
  num: 32,
  slug: "players-only-meetings",
  title: "Players-Only Meetings",
  category: "Leadership & Culture",
  palette: "sand",
  hero: { layout: "agenda-ladder", concept: "A narrow upright ladder of three rungs, climbed from the bottom: purpose, rules, close. The top rung, one action with one owner, is the one most often skipped." },
  compose: { hook: "question-only", takeaway: "question-right", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "A players-only meeting works when it has a purpose, a few rules and a clear close, set up by staff and then run by the players.",
  question: "Who on our teams could run that meeting well tomorrow?",
  caveats: `There are no studies that test players-only meetings directly. Say so. The ladder borrows from research on workplace team meetings (agendas, functional vs. complaining talk) and from team psychology (psychological safety, athlete leadership), plus coaching experience. Present it as a sensible structure, not a proven protocol.

Never promise that "what's said here stays here." Staff remain bound by reporting duties: hazing, Title IX, threats of harm, anything touching an athlete's safety or welfare. The leaders running the meeting should know that before it starts, and know which staff member to bring anything serious to. Anything clinical goes to Baylor's licensed mental health staff.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "kauffeld2012", cite: "Kauffeld S, Lehmann-Willenbrock N. Meetings matter: effects of team meetings on team and organizational success. Small Group Res. 2012;43(2):130-158. doi:10.1177/1046496411429599", grade: "observational study of 92 videotaped workplace team meetings", found: "external", supports: "Problem-solving talk and action planning went with more satisfying meetings and higher team productivity; dysfunctional talk such as criticizing and complaining showed negative relationships with these outcomes." },
    { id: "leach2009", cite: "Leach DJ, Rogelberg SG, Warr PB, Burnfield JL. Perceived meeting effectiveness: the role of design characteristics. J Bus Psychol. 2009;24(1):65-76. doi:10.1007/s10869-009-9092-6", grade: "cross-sectional survey of employees", found: "external", supports: "Agenda use, punctuality and facility quality were associated with meetings being rated more effective." },
    { id: "edmondson1999", cite: "Edmondson A. Psychological safety and learning behavior in work teams. Adm Sci Q. 1999;44(2):350-383. doi:10.2307/2666999", grade: "multimethod field study of 51 work teams", found: "external", supports: "Team psychological safety, a shared belief that the team is safe for interpersonal risk taking, was associated with learning behavior, which in turn related to team performance." },
    { id: "fransen2020", cite: "Fransen K, Haslam SA, Steffens NK, Mallett CJ, Peters K, Boen F. Making 'us' better: high-quality athlete leadership relates to health and burnout in professional Australian football teams. Eur J Sport Sci. 2020;20(7):953-963. doi:10.1080/17461391.2019.1680736", grade: "cross-sectional study (120 professional athletes, three teams)", found: "external", supports: "Good athlete leaders were associated with better teammate health and lower burnout, through a stronger shared team identity." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Every coach in this room has either called for one, been told about one afterward, or heard about one going sideways. The players-only meeting.

Sometimes it's the turning point of a season. Sometimes it's where a locker room splits. Today is about what makes the difference, and what staff can do before they walk out of the room.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("What happens after the coaches leave the room?", { y: 1.85, w: 10.2, h: 2.75, size: 82 });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Honest starting point: nobody has run a trial on players-only meetings. What we have is research on team meetings at work, research on how teams talk, and a lot of experience.

The pattern is familiar. The meeting gets called after a bad loss, with no plan. The loudest voices take over and it turns into a complaint session.

The workplace research lines up with that. When researchers videotaped ninety-two team meetings, problem-solving and action planning went with better meetings and more productive teams. Criticizing and complaining went the other way. A meeting with no structure drifts toward the second kind.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Too often they’re called in a crisis, with no plan.", { y: 1.2, w: 8.4, h: 1.75, size: 52 });
        s.label("What tends to happen", { x: 0.75, y: 3.55, w: 5.2 });
        s.text(0.75, 3.95, 5.2, 1.6, "The loudest voices take over, and it turns into complaining.", { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.18 });
        s.label("What meeting research finds", { x: 6.9, y: 3.55, w: 5.6 });
        s.text(6.9, 3.95, 5.6, 1.6, "Complaining and criticizing go with worse team results.", { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.18 });
        s.source("Workplace teams: Kauffeld & Lehmann-Willenbrock 2012", { refs: ["kauffeld2012"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So give it a shape. Think of a ladder with three rungs, climbed from the bottom.

Purpose first: one question the meeting is there to answer. Not "what's wrong with us," but something like "what do we need from each other this week?" Meetings with a clear agenda get rated as more effective.

Rules second: one voice at a time, no personal attacks. That's what makes it safe for the quiet player to speak. Teams that feel safe to take that risk learn faster.

Close last, and it's the rung people skip: one agreed action, with one owner. That's what turns talk into something the team can see on Tuesday.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Structure is what makes it safe to speak up.", { y: 1.25, w: 6.6, h: 2.85, size: 58 });
        const xa = 8.15, xb = 8.85, top = 2.15, bot = 6.05;
        s.line(xa, top, xa, bot, { color: "muted", w: 1.5 });
        s.line(xb, top, xb, bot, { color: "muted", w: 1.5 });
        const rungs = [
          { y: 5.45, name: "Purpose", t: "One question to answer." },
          { y: 4.1, name: "Rules", t: "One voice at a time. No attacks." },
          { y: 2.75, name: "Close", t: "One action, one owner.", hot: true },
        ];
        rungs.forEach(r => {
          s.line(xa, r.y, xb, r.y, { color: r.hot ? "accent" : "muted", w: r.hot ? 5 : 2.5 });
          s.text(9.2, r.y - 0.5, 3.4, 0.45, r.name, { font: "condBold", size: 26, color: r.hot ? "accent" : "ink" });
          s.text(9.2, r.y - 0.02, 3.4, 0.7, r.t, { font: "light", size: 18, color: "muted", lineH: 1.15 });
        });
        s.source("Leach et al. 2009 · Edmondson 1999 · Kauffeld & Lehmann-Willenbrock 2012", { y: 6.2, w: 7, refs: ["leach2009", "edmondson1999", "kauffeld2012"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `Here's where staff come in, and the order matters.

First, before the meeting: the player leaders sit down with one staff member and agree on the question. Ten minutes. This is also when they hear the one rule staff can't bend: anything about safety, hazing or someone being hurt comes to us.

Second, during: staff leave. The leaders run the three rungs. This is their meeting.

Third, after: the leaders bring back the action, not a transcript. Nobody needs to know who said what. Good athlete leaders are linked with healthier, less burned-out teams, and this is one of the ways they get built.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Staff set it up, then step out.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.8, size: 44 });
        const steps = [
          ["1", "Before: leaders and one staff member agree on the question."],
          ["2", "During: staff leave. Leaders run the three rungs."],
          ["3", "After: leaders bring back the action, not a transcript."],
        ];
        steps.forEach(([k, t], i) => {
          const y = 2.45 + i * 1.18;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.22, 0.8, 0.7, k, { font: "cond", size: 36, color: "muted", lineH: 0.9 });
          s.text(1.75, y + 0.26, 10.8, 0.6, t, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.1 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 2.45 + 3 * 1.18, 12.58, 2.45 + 3 * 1.18, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.source("Fransen et al., Eur J Sport Sci, 2020", { y: 6.2, refs: ["fransen2020"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: give them the room, and give them a plan. A purpose, a couple of rules, and a close.

Question for the room: who on our teams could run that meeting well tomorrow? And if the answer is "no one yet," that's useful too. It tells us whose leadership we should be building this year.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Give them the room and a plan.", { y: 1.3, w: 7.6, h: 2.3, size: 72 });
        s.text(12.58 - 6.2, 4.3, 6.2, 1.6, "Who on our teams could run that meeting well tomorrow?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

